{
  "lens": "Decision completeness and fixed-limit floors. One independent reading of the supplied repair, parent design, principle, diff and preflight helper. No cross-family corroboration or test execution.",
  "verdict": "CHANGES_REQUIRED",
  "findings": [
    {
      "class": "DESIGN",
      "location": "Compatibility and rollback; MessageRouter.relay, recoverPendingRelayed and the parent D.2 boot path",
      "finding": "There is a downgrade window before the first pin. A new receiver can durably accept a bound envelope as received with deliveryPending=true, acknowledge it, and stop before resolving it. The parent receiver recovers exactly that state by resolving the name. It does not understand the signed sessionId. The new receiver also stored arrival−age without the legacy subtraction for this envelope. A replacement that started in transit can pass that cutoff. The same issue arises if a peer proves support, then downgrades before payload arrival. A support check at enablement cannot fence either transition. Give bound payloads a capability-specific admission path that old receivers cannot accept, and give newly accepted, unpinned bound work an on-disk state old recovery cannot inject. Exercise both version transitions with a replacement."
    },
    {
      "class": "DESIGN",
      "location": "DeliveryRetryManager.processRetries, beginDeliveryAttempt; MessageRouter.deliverSameAgentLocal",
      "finding": "A retry tick takes the message claim before it knows whether it has work. It can claim a newly stored received, deliveryPending envelope with no typingStartedAt and no holderPending. The initial delivery then sees a busy claim and returns. The tick has no branch to execute this initial delivery, and subsequent ticks also skip it until expiry. Boot recovery can lose the same race. Assign an executor to this state under the claim, or make a losing initial executor recheck after release. Include a test where the tick wins before the router's first read."
    },
    {
      "class": "DESIGN",
      "location": "MessageRouter.send, generationLanes finally block",
      "finding": "An expired last waiter deletes the map entry while its predecessor can still own the lane. For example, A is still saving its send, B times out and records its failure, then C finds an empty map and starts concurrently with A. Retire the map entry only after its predecessor chain has actually settled. An expired waiter can report failure immediately without releasing another sender's admission."
    },
    {
      "class": "DESIGN",
      "location": "MessageRouter.send and sendMessage; AgentMessage.to.machine",
      "finding": "The preflight predicate treats machine='*' with a named session as one named peer. The documented address means all machines. With the flag on, the code looks up a machine literally named '*' and can fail the send before its existing routing policy runs. Bind only a concrete machine, or explicitly design per-recipient binding for fan-out. Classify the normalized destination once, including legacy unset/local forms. Strip caller-supplied reserved fields at ingress for every destination, as promised, rather than only inside the named-peer branch."
    },
    {
      "class": "DESIGN",
      "location": "MessageDelivery.deliverToSession; DeliveryRetryManager pinned checks and generic queued path",
      "finding": "The last synchronous check fails open for a persisted legacy pin when isSessionGeneration is absent. An earlier isPinnedSessionLive result can go stale during the safety await. Also, a pre-upgrade queued envelope with neither holderPending nor a pin takes the direct to.session path in the shown retry code, despite parent B requiring resolve-once-and-pin. Route unpinned named recipient work through the resolver before typing, and require a current generation check for every pin. A missing dependency should produce a recorded no-injection result."
    },
    {
      "class": "DESIGN",
      "location": "MessageRouter.doDeliverSameAgentLocal pin write; DeliveryRetryManager retry arming; Frontloaded Decision 5",
      "finding": "The retry manager sets phase='received' in its arming write, but the router's pin write preserves the previous phase. A direct local send can therefore be armed in sent, contrary to the stated old-reader recovery contract. The parent boot path explicitly handles only received. Write the downgrade-safe phase with the pin and marker in every injection path. A pin-write failure also returns after mutating the in-memory envelope without establishing that a direct sent record has any retry owner. Define its durable outcome and verify the returned phase against the store."
    },
    {
      "class": "DESIGN",
      "location": "MessageRouter.currentSessionGeneration; machineRoutes generation endpoint; parent C and D.7",
      "finding": "Preflight collapses ambiguous, unknown, ended-job, foreign placement, known-but-empty and missing resolver into signed null. Enabled sends consequently fail at origin with the same 'no unique running session' explanation. In particular, the sender loses the destination machine for a moved topic and the candidate names for ambiguity. Preserve these existing actionable outcomes in a signed, bounded refusal, or frontload the decision to remove them. Missing resolver is an unavailable check, not an observation that there is no holder."
    },
    {
      "class": "DESIGN",
      "location": "layer2RetryDecision; router initialization of holderRetryState; retry manager give-up branch",
      "finding": "The inherited constants do not reproduce the inherited opportunities. lastAttemptAt=0 makes the first retry eligible on the next 15-second tick. From that retry the ideal subsequent offsets are 30, 90 and 210 seconds. The following offset is 450 seconds, outside the 300-second window. A legitimate holder first visible at 240 seconds is never checked again, even though the work window remains open. The store nevertheless says 'max retries exceeded (10)'. Decide whether a final eligible check is intended, define whether the initial refusal counts, and persist the actual stop reason and count. A finite breaker is justified independently of these values."
    },
    {
      "class": "DESIGN",
      "location": "DeliveryRetryManager result handling and watchdog; MessageRouter.recordGenerationEvidence; maturation plan",
      "finding": "Pinned retry successes, pinned retry uncertainty, pre-injection generation changes and recovery of armed attempts can bypass the router's evidence calls. The rollout owner can see zero uncertainty events while an envelope actually ended outcome unknown. Record evidence from the durable outcome across all executors and retain a distinct changed-generation reason at the final check. The diff also explicitly registers a watchdog after successful injection and mentions regression to queued. Its implementation is absent. Require evidence that a missing observation or ACK after a confirmed paste cannot cause another paste of that id."
    },
    {
      "class": "DESIGN",
      "location": "Terminal persistence followed by reportTerminalFailure/notifyDeadLetter; server.ts onDeliveryFailure; parent C notice recovery",
      "finding": "A crash after the terminal write and before the notifier durably records pending work leaves a failed envelope that later recovery skips. The shown callback is fire-and-forget. Conversely, a notifier that pastes a notice and crashes before marking it sent cannot infer that another paste is safe. Persist notification intent with the terminal outcome, distinguish one logical notice from guaranteed physical display, and specify recovery of an uncertain notice. The supplied notifier and store implementations are insufficient to establish that contract."
    },
    {
      "class": "DESIGN",
      "location": "MessageRouter.auditRefusal; Fixed behavioral limits",
      "finding": "The writer has no demonstrated byte limit on a serialized row. It checks existing size with '>' before appending, without accounting for the incoming row. A file at the threshold accepts another complete row, and a long legacy target can dominate both retention and logging latency. The 200-unit preflight check does not establish an all-ingress bound. Bound evidence fields or the encoded row, keep a lookup key for full detail, and rotate against projected bytes. Decide whether slow audit I/O may delay the durable refusal and every subsequent audit caller."
    },
    {
      "class": "PRECISION",
      "location": "sessionAddressResolver.describeHolderRefusal; types.ts asOf; MessageRouter relay and retry comments",
      "finding": "Legacy asOf is a conservative reference estimate. The notice says the message was written at that timestamp and calls the holder a replacement, even for the incident's genuinely older holder. Report the cutoff and the observed start separately. Several comments also describe holderPending as queued or describe the legacy subtraction as removed, while the code uses received and retains the subtraction."
    },
    {
      "class": "PRECISION",
      "location": "Verification and current status; Evidence at decision boundaries",
      "finding": "The supplied evidence is design text and partial source. It omits the actual old binaries, full watchdog, store and notifier, cache invalidation, auth/signature serializers, API ingress normalization, transport guard, tmux adapter, self-action registry, awareness/ladder changes, tests and results. In particular, listRunningSessions().some(...) establishes a synchronous lookup, but the parent documents a one-second cache. Freshness at injection needs evidence of invalidation or an authoritative uncached read. No live, downgrade, or test:all result was independently reviewed."
    }
  ],
  "fixedLimits": [
    {
      "location": "Signed generation before creation; endpoint auth and digest",
      "claimedBasis": "One authenticated active same-agent receiver, one fresh request nonce, one unique id before createdAt; SHA-256 over the three ordered fields, v1 domain tag, Ed25519 relay ACK and pinned peer key.",
      "independentEvidence": "A nonce from another request, a changed tuple or an answer from another peer cannot establish the requested observation. The helper recomputes the digest and supplies expected peer and nonce to verification. The route checks isMachineActive.",
      "legitimateRejectedCounterexample": "An authentic observation from a previous request can still describe the same running session. Its continued accuracy is unknowable from that response.",
      "verdict": "Invariant for authenticated identity, request binding and pre-creation ordering. The exact serialization and primitives are protocol choices fixed for v1 interoperability. The underlying signer, verifier and revocation predicate were not supplied."
    },
    {
      "location": "Preflight scope and machineRoutes target validation",
      "claimedBasis": "Named same-agent cross-machine sessions only; nonempty string, maximum 200 characters; exclude '*' and 'best'.",
      "independentEvidence": "A broadcast or routing directive does not identify one generation. The route uses JavaScript length, measured in UTF-16 code units. No existing session-name API validator was supplied.",
      "legitimateRejectedCounterexample": "A real name with 101 supplementary Unicode characters exceeds 200 code units. A named target on machine='*' is also a documented address.",
      "verdict": "Invariant that one proof needs one concrete target and peer. The length and supported address forms are compatibility judgments. Verify the same validator and normalized routing scope at both ends."
    },
    {
      "location": "readPeerMessageGeneration observation validation and boundedGenerationBody",
      "claimedBasis": "Non-null id length 1–200, UUID ids, safe-integer observedAt and maximum 4096 response bytes before parsing.",
      "independentEvidence": "A normal UUID fits easily. The helper accepts arbitrary nonempty ids within the limit and does not compare clocks. JSON escaping, machine-id encoding, signature representation and whitespace affect wire size; their complete bounds are absent.",
      "legitimateRejectedCounterexample": "A correctly signed compact observation in a response padded with harmless JSON whitespace can exceed 4096 bytes.",
      "verdict": "Invariant for unambiguous field types and bounded parsing. Exact id and body limits are judgment candidates until the canonical producer's maximum encoded output is established. observedAt is signed metadata only."
    },
    {
      "location": "Preflight addresses, redirect policy and proof settlement",
      "claimedBasis": "One pass over distinct registered HTTP/HTTPS URL strings, zero followed redirects, fresh nonce per address; only a verified positive or negative observation settles early.",
      "independentEvidence": "An unsigned 404, redirect or success can originate at an intermediary. Equal URL strings are deduplicated; equivalent URL spellings can still occupy separate slots. The parent payload path applies a credential guard while this helper checks schemes only.",
      "legitimateRejectedCounterexample": "A legitimate peer reachable only through an unregistered redirect cannot answer through this policy. Two registered aliases for one endpoint can reduce the time allocated to a working address.",
      "verdict": "Invariant for verified settlement and no implicit expansion of authorized destinations. Supported schemes, exact deduplication and a single pass are transport judgments. Registered-address provenance and allowed disclosure of signed request metadata are dependencies."
    },
    {
      "location": "MessageRouter.send; readPeerMessageGeneration timers",
      "claimedBasis": "One active preflight/send per peer; 5000 ms total including admission; equal share of remaining time per remaining address with an at-least-1-ms rounded timer.",
      "independentEvidence": "Bounding work and reserving time for alternatives serve availability. Neither proves five seconds or concurrency one is necessary. With two addresses the first can be cut off near 2.5 seconds. Address preparation precedes the helper deadline and ordinary store work can hold the lane beyond it.",
      "legitimateRejectedCounterexample": "A valid first peer answers in three seconds, the second address is dead, or an unrelated slow store write holds the lane ahead of a healthy peer.",
      "verdict": "Judgment candidates for concurrency, duration, split and rounding. Failure to obtain proof remains a hard no-send. The caller-visible five seconds is a proof/admission budget, not a bound on completing the send."
    },
    {
      "location": "Resolver rules; parent A and E; server.ts final generation check",
      "claimedBasis": "First deciding rule, exactly one running eligible candidate, zero standing-down candidates, exact/topic/name/prefix order, collapse one repeated prefix, retain historical claimants, one-second session-list cache.",
      "independentEvidence": "Two candidates cannot establish unique identity. A reused name can refer to a different id. A cached positive can survive a recorded replacement unless updates invalidate it. An existential name/id check also assumes a name cannot simultaneously belong to multiple running records.",
      "legitimateRejectedCounterexample": "Two plausible holders include the intended one; a role with a pruned or differently normalized history might also be identifiable by independent evidence.",
      "verdict": "Invariant for unique current ownership before injection. Lookup priority, cache TTL, naming aliases, numbered collision fallback and retention for the state directory's lifetime are inherited policy choices. Fresh final evidence and generation non-reuse are load-bearing."
    },
    {
      "location": "Legacy and bound asOf; parent D.3; currentSessionGeneration",
      "claimedBasis": "Five-minute signature window W, age clamped to [0,type TTL], missing age zero, legacy cutoff arrival−age−2W; bound lookup uses Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER. Git pickup without the clock check stores only.",
      "independentEvidence": "Clock offset can cancel transit delay. Subtracting ten minutes neither proves identity nor distinguishes the recorded young holder from a replacement. The large sentinel suppresses freshness for ordinary valid dates.",
      "legitimateRejectedCounterexample": "The incident's holder started before the message but inside the ten-minute exclusion. An eligible git-arrived message can also remain untyped.",
      "verdict": "Legacy compatibility judgments, including exact W and 2W. No-clock-comparison identity for bound delivery is an invariant. The sentinel is an implementation choice, not an authorization threshold."
    },
    {
      "location": "isHolderPendingRefusal and resolver refusal handling",
      "claimedBasis": "Wait only for legacy too-new or known no-holder/no-local-topic; changed or missing required id, ambiguity, foreign placement, ended job, unknown name and resolver exceptions terminate.",
      "independentEvidence": "The union enumerates observations, not future states. A temporarily missing historical record looks unknown, a resolver outage can recover, and ambiguity may clear while the signed id stays the same.",
      "legitimateRejectedCounterexample": "A bound target is briefly ambiguous or its history is temporarily unavailable, then the original signed generation becomes uniquely visible before TTL.",
      "verdict": "Invariant against substituting an id or inventing missing pre-send proof. Wait versus terminal for inconclusive observations is a judgment candidate. No forwarding and immediate failure on a verified replacement are explicit scope decisions."
    },
    {
      "location": "Pin, physical attempt and recovery boundaries",
      "claimedBasis": "One persisted pin before typing, one active attempt per immutable id, final synchronous generation check after the last await, zero automatic retypes after possible injection; busy-before-injection may retry.",
      "independentEvidence": "A false return or exception can follow pasted chunks or Enter. A durable marker can also precede a crash before the first byte. An in-memory Map covers only one process; physical tmux replacement outside the records is expressly excluded.",
      "legitimateRejectedCounterexample": "A crash immediately after writing the marker can suppress a message that in fact never reached tmux.",
      "verdict": "Invariant for no repeat after uncertainty and no generation substitution. Phase representation and in-process locking are implementation choices requiring exclusive store ownership or equivalent interprocess coordination. A retryable result must prove zero injection."
    },
    {
      "location": "Layer-2 retry constants, tick, TTL and retained generic policy",
      "claimedBasis": "10 retries, 30-second base, doubling with exponent max(0, attempts−1), 300-second interval cap and work window, 15-second tick, earlier type/message TTL, state retained across restart. Generic lane keeps fixed cadence.",
      "independentEvidence": "The product 10×30 seconds describes the old fixed cadence. With exponential backoff, ideal retry times relative to the first retry are 0, 30, 90 and 210 seconds. Date.now() also makes elapsed eligibility sensitive to wall-clock changes.",
      "legitimateRejectedCounterexample": "A holder visible from second 240 onward could be reached within five minutes by one final check. A forward clock correction can terminate an otherwise eligible wait early.",
      "verdict": "Invariant for finite durable work, honoring expiry, and retaining a pin. Every exact number, multiplier, tick, first-retry timing and choice of time source is a judgment candidate. Type TTL values and caller override validation are absent. The adjacent Layer-1 30-minute interval cap and four-hour window are unchanged and provide no floor for this lane."
    },
    {
      "location": "Parent D relay and remote failure notices",
      "claimedBasis": "One ordered queue per target, one request at a time, one pass, 10 seconds per address, maximum nominal 10 seconds times address count; one remote notice pass, no forwarding, permanent legacy path alias.",
      "independentEvidence": "Retrying the same immutable id across addresses relies on receiver dedup, including dead letters. A timeout cannot establish non-arrival. A remote notice may be lost even after the payload was accepted.",
      "legitimateRejectedCounterexample": "A peer returns just after the pass, or the origin comes back immediately after the only notice pass.",
      "verdict": "Judgment candidates for timing, queue shape, pass count, alias lifetime and phase-one reach. Invariants for authenticated ACK/NACK, idempotent duplicate handling, and preserving uncertainty across later address results. Existing type-specific ACK, TTL and late-notice hold durations were not supplied."
    },
